<p class="prose">Eight months ago, Iran and the United States went to war, and the Strait of Hormuz has been substantially closed, mined, or under direct attack ever since. The Black Sea has been a live shipping hazard for longer than that. This is now not just about war risk premiums and tanker schedules; it's affecting public policy and customer behaviour.</p>

<p class="prose">Some countries are releasing national strategic reserves to contain prices, offering emergency tax cuts, price caps supported by government funds and advice for consumers to stock up.</p>

<p class="prose">The relative exposure from the Narrows Chokepoint Dependency Monitor testifies to the urgency each government has demonstrated in intervening to keep that exposure from reaching ordinary people. The size of that intervention tracks pretty well with the bilateral Hormuz dependency figures in our model. The countries fretting the most have the most to lose.</p>

