<p class="prose">Japan's government has leaned on this harder than almost anyone. In March, Tokyo made its first release of state oil reserves in four years, followed by a second release in April. The trade ministry has since convened panels on subsidising alternative shipping routes to avoid Hormuz. By August, the Takaichi government was burning through reserve funds specifically to hold the nationwide pump price near 170 yen per litre. Japan's CDM figure explains the urgency: 79.1 percent of its crude imports depend on Hormuz, the highest of any major importer, worth 56.8 billion dollars a year in exposure.</p>

