<p class="prose">South Korea's response has been faster and more visibly strained. In the first five days of March, national average gasoline rose 6.7 percent and diesel 11.7 percent, the first time pump prices had cleared 1,800 won per litre since 2022. The government's own state energy research institute, KEEI, told officials to prepare for an outright energy crisis in LNG and oil supply. Seoul cut energy taxes to soften the blow, and by April, even after that relief, gasoline and diesel were still climbing, with seven tankers, four of them Korean-operated, sitting idle in the strait. Korea's CDM dependency is 63.9 percent, 54.6 billion dollars at risk, the fourth-highest absolute figure in the full 31-country dataset, behind China, India, and Japan.</p>

<p class="prose">New Zealand's dependency percentage, 72.5 percent of crude runs through Hormuz, sits second only to Japan's among the countries in this piece, yet its dollar value at risk, 384 million dollars, is the smallest of the five by a wide margin, because New Zealand's economy is small. That combination, a dependency share near the top of the model paired with the thinnest absolute buffer of any country on this list, is the profile that produces blunt public warnings. The Automobile Association has told drivers that petrol prices could reach four dollars a litre and that they ought to fill up before it does.</p>

<p class="prose">Australia's own bilateral exposure to Hormuz is modest. Just 5.5 percent of its crude imports transit the strait, 315 million dollars at risk, nowhere near Japan's 79.1 percent. Most of Australia's crude comes from Malaysia, the United States, Vietnam, and Brunei instead.</p>

<p class="prose">Fertiliser is a more complicated picture. Counting the full basket of nitrogenous, ammonia, phosphatic, potassic and compound N/P/K fertilisers, including the Saudi-sourced DAP and MAP that a nitrogenous-only count would miss, 1.055 billion dollars of Australia's fertiliser imports transit Hormuz: 27.6 percent of the total, against 789 million dollars and 38.9 percent on the nitrogenous-only count. DAP and MAP alone account for the extra 266 million dollars. The percentage still fell, even as the dollar figure rose, because the full basket is nearly twice the size the nitrogenous-only figure implied, 3.8 billion dollars a year rather than 2.0 billion, and most of that added volume, potash and straight phosphate, is barely sourced from the Gulf at all. More dollars at risk, a smaller share of a bigger market: both are true at once.</p>

<p class="prose">Despite that relatively low dependency, Australian petrol prices still climbed hard, from 1.57 to 2.07 dollars a litre in eight weeks in March, reaching 2.11 dollars by September, up from 1.55 in July, with diesel up roughly 50 percent since June. Canberra cut the fuel excise to ease the burden, then adjusted it, then let it lapse.</p>

<p class="prose">A federal minister has named the Strait of Hormuz as the reason why prices have risen, but this is a bit tricky. Australia has quite a low bilateral dependency, but Australia buys refined fuel based on regional benchmark pricing in Singapore rather than relying on fixed bilateral contracts, so a Hormuz disruption lifts that benchmark price for every buyer on it, regardless of whose tanker the fuel actually arrived on. The pump price moves with the conflict even when the trade data proves it does not have to.</p>

