<p class="prose">Fertiliser is really three different commodities, and Hormuz matters to each differently. Urea &mdash; the nitrogen fertiliser Australian grain growers rely on most &mdash; had 43 percent of its global trade passing through the strait before the war, more than twice crude oil's 20 percent share; sulphur, the feedstock for phosphate fertiliser, was at 44 percent; anhydrous ammonia averaged 27 percent between 2019 and 2023. Australia's own dependence compounds this: between 2019 and 2023 the country imported close to 90 percent of its nitrogen fertiliser, nearly 70 percent of its phosphate, and the entirety of its potash. Australia's Hormuz-linked fertiliser exposure rose in dollar terms, because DAP and MAP, both overwhelmingly Saudi-sourced, add $266 million of Gulf-origin trade. It fell as a share of the total, because potash and straight phosphate add $371 million to the denominator while being almost entirely non-Gulf-sourced.</p>

<p class="prose">Qatar's cheap gas makes it the world's largest urea producer, and more than 60 percent of Australia's urea imports come from the Middle East; an Iranian missile strike on a major Qatari facility in March knocked out a significant share of that supply, and Middle Eastern urea prices have nearly doubled in Australian-dollar terms since the start of the year. Australia has not produced its own urea since Incitec Pivot closed its Gibson Island plant in Brisbane in December 2022; a replacement, the company's Perdaman project in Karratha, Western Australia, is due online in mid-2027 and would meet around 90 percent of domestic urea demand, putting a known end date on the exposure. In the meantime, the Commonwealth Bank estimates fuel, fertiliser and road transport make up about 14 percent of total farm input costs (diesel 6 percent, fertiliser 5 percent), both now running well above their five-year averages &mdash; diesel up 79 percent, fertiliser up 67 percent &mdash; and models a worst case in which a 45 percent cut to fertiliser use trims wheat output by a quarter and barley and canola by more than 30 percent. Anthony Albanese returned from a regional tour with an extra 250,000 tonnes of Indonesian urea, enough to cover roughly a fifth of the season's remaining need.</p>

