<p class="prose">Thailand's government moved earliest and most aggressively. In March, after panic buying at the pumps, Bangkok froze diesel prices, ordered the state Oil Fuel Fund to subsidise diesel to hold a 30 baht ceiling, suspended fuel exports outside the region, and tripled its mandatory reserve requirement from one to three percent of consumption. Officials had ninety-five days of cover at the time. By September, the Oil Fuel Fund's deficit had swollen past 80 billion baht and the energy ministry was warning prices would stay volatile as the US-Iran conflict reignited. As of this month, Bangkok pump prices sit at 50.43 baht a litre for petrol and 42.94 baht for diesel, but only because of continuous government intervention. Thailand's CDM dependency is 45.6 percent, 15.3 billion dollars at risk, moderate by regional standards, yet still enough to force the earliest state intervention of any country in this set.</p>

<p class="prose">After eight months, this conflict has stressed the resilience of economies across Asia and toward the Pacific. For Japan, Korea, Thailand, and New Zealand, the order in which governments reached for solutions tracks the dependency ranking closely. The highest-dependency major economies reached for reserves and emergency panels. The others, each exposed in their own way, reached for price caps and blunt consumer warnings.</p>

